     1	# Pitch 9 — Buy the certified-inspection microfirm and keep the certificate-holder (Family B)
     2	
     3	**The unit and the file.** One microbusiness whose product is a signed inspection that the law
     4	requires and that only a certified person may sign: an asbestos survey firm (the DIA surveyor),
     5	an emergency-generator inspection firm (SCIOS scope 4/7c), a pest-control firm (per-person
     6	biocide competence). Sells inspections per site or per contract; the firm's value is the
     7	certificate-holder's signature and the contract book.
     8	
     9	**The rule and the date.** Asbestos surveys mandatory before renovation and demolition; SCIOS
    10	generator inspection mandatory since 1 January 2016 on a two-year cycle above 100 kW (verified,
    11	AI-footprint memo); biocide use requires per-person competence (verified). No new date; the
    12	obligations are standing, which is the point: they do not expire.
    13	
    14	**Who buys.** Building owners, contractors, data-centre and hospital facility managers, food and
    15	housing operators. Demand is regulatory and steady; the incumbents are small and ageing; the pest
    16	listing found shows about €240k revenue and €78k EBITDA on two to five staff, inside the
    17	€100k-plus-Qredits envelope at the barometer's 3.6× (verified, boring-business memo).
    18	
    19	**Why you.** Capital and the owner-independence rule from the succession memo; process discipline
    20	for scheduling, evidence and reporting that these firms run on paper; CEE-speaking clients in
    21	housing and food for pest control. The Dutch gate is the exam, which is Dutch-only in all three
    22	cases and which the reader does not take: the certificate-holder stays, on paper, through the
    23	change of control.
    24	
    25	**The precedent.** The class is the precedent: three memos found the same deal shape in three
    26	sectors. One listing (pest control, Brookz) is saved; the asbestos memo found a firm seeking
    27	freelance surveyors in three cities (verified).
    28	
    29	**The ninety-day test.** One overflow job brokered to a certified firm for a fee: proves the
    30	demand and the relationship before any purchase. Under €200.
    31	
    32	**Steps two and three.** Step two: buy one firm with the certificate-holder on a retention
    33	agreement. Step three: a second certificate-holder hired, or a second firm in an adjacent
    34	inspection class on the same back office; the AI build-out adds generator fleets as a customer.
    35	
    36	**The AI line.** A survey is a person on a site with a sampling kit and a signature; AI writes
    37	the report. Demand rises with regulation, and regulation is not falling.
    38	
    39	**The kill fact.** No certificate-holder stays through a change of control at a price the
    40	capital allows. It is answered in the first conversation with the seller.
    41	
    42	**The number to settle first.** The retention cost of the certificate-holder against the firm's
    43	EBITDA: if the signature costs half the profit, there is no deal.
    44	
    45	**Verdict under the four assumptions.** Ranked ninth. Not beside a job after purchase; capital
    46	is most of the ceiling; no retraining, because the reader never takes the exam. Rises if the
    47	capital ceiling is higher; fails if the reader wants to be the one who signs.

